+# Review: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockShop.server.luau`
+
+Findings are ordered by impact. Line numbers refer to the script as of commit
+`f935ed5`. Items 1 to 3 are exploits or broken features and should be fixed
+before building on this script. Items 4 to 6 are bugs or design gaps. Items 7
+to 10 are robustness, API shape, and style.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Client arguments are trusted. One NaN gives a player unlimited free blocks forever
+
+**Where:** `BuyBlocks` handler (lines 84-103), `PlaceBlock` handler
+(lines 105-125), `RemoveBlock` handler (lines 127-140).
+
+**What's wrong:** The handlers annotate client values as `string`, `number`,
+`Vector3`, and `Instance`. Those annotations only tell the type checker to
+trust the client. A client can send anything.
+
+- **`amt = 0/0` (NaN) in `BuyBlocks`.** Every comparison with NaN is false,
+  so `amt < 1 or amt > MAX_PURCHASE` lets it through. `price` becomes NaN,
+  `coins < price` is false, and `setCoins` writes **NaN to the DataStore**.
+  After that, `coins < price` stays false on every later purchase, so the
+  player can buy as much as they want, permanently. Their stock becomes NaN
+  too, and `NaN < 1` is false, so the stock check in `PlaceBlock` passes
+  forever as well.
+- **Fractional `amt`** (for example `1.5`) is accepted. It charges fractional
+  coins and grants fractional stock.
+- **Unknown or non-string `bt`** makes `PRICES[bt] * amt` error. **A string
+  `amt`** makes the comparison error. Neither can be exploited, but any client
+  can use them to spam errors.
+- **NaN components in `pos` in `PlaceBlock`.** `PlacementRules.snap` returns a
+  NaN position. `PlacementRules.check` then passes it, because the height
+  check (`center.Y < 0`, `> MaxHeight`) and the reach check
+  (`Magnitude > MaxReach`) are both false for NaN. The block is placed at a
+  NaN position, from any distance. A non-`Vector3` `pos` errors inside
+  `snap`.
+- **`part = nil` in `RemoveBlock`** errors on `part.Parent`.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type every argument after `player` as `unknown`.
+Narrow each one with `typeof` and range checks that are shaped to reject NaN,
+and return early when a check fails:
+
+```lua
+buyRemote.OnServerEvent:Connect(function(player: Player, blockType: unknown, amount: unknown)
+	if not BlockTypes.isBlockType(blockType) then
+		return
+	end
+	if
+		typeof(amount) ~= "number"
+		or amount ~= math.floor(amount) -- rejects NaN and fractions
+		or amount < 1
+		or amount > MAX_PURCHASE
+	then
+		return
+	end
+	-- blockType :: BlockTypes.BlockType, amount is a whole number in range
+end)
+```
+
+For `pos`, check `typeof(pos) == "Vector3"` and that all three components are
+finite (`x == x and math.abs(x) ~= math.huge`). Consider adding the same
+finite check to `PlacementRules.check` too, so the client preview and the
+server agree on it. For `part`, check `typeof(part) == "Instance"` and
+`part:IsA("BasePart")`.
+
+Make `BlockTypes.isBlockType` narrow its argument
+(`(value: unknown) -> boolean` cannot narrow, so cast after the check, or
+change it to return `BlockType?`). That also removes the
+`bt :: BlockTypes.BlockType` cast at line 66.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Coins are read and written in the DataStore on every purchase: duplication race, throttling, unhandled errors
+
+**Where:** `getCoins` / `setCoins` (lines 42-48), used at lines 90-94.
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **Race / duplication.** `GetAsync` yields. If a client fires `BuyBlocks`
+  twice in quick succession, both handlers read the same balance, both pass
+  `coins < price`, and both write `coins - price`. The player pays once and
+  gets the stock twice. Repeating this lets them buy without limit.
+- **Throttling.** Each purchase costs one read and one write on the same key.
+  DataStore budgets are per server, and writes to the same key have a
+  cooldown. A single player spamming the remote can use up the budget, which
+  delays or fails saves for everyone on the server.
+- **No error handling.** `GetAsync` and `SetAsync` throw on network or
+  service failure. Nothing is wrapped in `pcall`, so failures show up as
+  random handler errors with no retry and no feedback to the player.
+- **Yields are hidden.** Neither function has an `Async` suffix, so nothing at
+  the call site shows that the handler yields partway through and can
+  interleave with other events. That interleaving is exactly what the race
+  above needs.
+
+**What to do instead:** Load the balance once when the player joins (in a
+`pcall`, with retries), keep it in a server-side table, and change it there
+synchronously. In-memory reads and writes don't yield, so the check-and-deduct
+can't interleave. Save on `PlayerRemoving`, in `game:BindToClose`, and on a
+periodic autosave, using `UpdateAsync` in a `pcall`. If coins are going to
+matter beyond this shop, use a session-locking data library (ProfileStore or
+similar) rather than writing your own. Also move coins out of this script
+into a module that owns player data, so the shop asks it to spend coins
+(`trySpend(player, price): boolean`) and doesn't touch storage itself.
+
+---
+
+## 3. `RemoveBlock`: abandoned blocks can never be removed, and the design refunds the wrong player
+
+**Where:** `getOwnerName` (lines 50-57) and the `RemoveBlock` handler
+(lines 127-140).
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- **The "owner has left" path always errors.** When no player matches,
+  `getOwnerName` falls off its end, which returns *no values*, not `nil`.
+  `tostring(getOwnerName(part))` then fails with "missing argument #1 to
+  'tostring'". So the feature the comment on line 132 describes (clearing a
+  block whose owner has left) can never happen.
+- **If that worked, it would be an exploit.** The refund on line 139 goes to
+  `stock[plr]`, the player who *removed* the block, not the owner. Clearing an
+  absent player's build would give the clearer free blocks.
+- **Ownership is compared by name through a string sentinel.** Comparing
+  `tostring(...)` against `"nil"` is fragile, and `"nil"` is a legal
+  three-character Roblox username. The attribute already holds a `UserId`, so
+  compare that.
+- **The refund reads the block type from `part.Name`.** The name is a display
+  property. A dedicated attribute is safer.
+- **There is no reach check.** A player can remove their own blocks from
+  anywhere on the map. That may be acceptable, but placing has a reach limit
+  and removing doesn't.
+
+**What to do instead:** Compare `UserId`s directly, and only refund the
+owner:
+
+```lua
+local ownerId = part:GetAttribute("OwnerId")
+local isOwner = ownerId == player.UserId
+local isOwnerPresent = typeof(ownerId) == "number"
+	and Players:GetPlayerByUserId(ownerId) ~= nil
+if not isOwner and isOwnerPresent then
+	return
+end
+
+part:Destroy()
+if isOwner then
+	-- refund to player
+end
+```
+
+Store the block type in a `BlockType` attribute in `placeBlock` and read it
+back here. Decide whether removing a block needs the same reach rule as
+placing one.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Purchased stock is lost when the player leaves, but the coins are already gone
+
+**Where:** `stock` (line 40), `PlayerRemoving` (lines 146-148).
+
+**What's wrong:** Coins are saved, but stock lives only in memory and is
+cleared on leave. A player who buys 50 blocks and disconnects (or whose
+server shuts down) loses them, and the coins don't come back. Their placed
+blocks also stay in `workspace` with no owner online. Once item 3 is fixed,
+anyone can clear them, and nothing ever cleans them up on its own.
+
+**What to do instead:** This is a product decision, but the two halves need
+to agree. Either save stock alongside coins (same load, save, and
+`BindToClose` path as item 2), or refund unplaced stock on leave. Decide what
+happens to a leaving player's placed blocks: destroy them on
+`PlayerRemoving`, refund them, or save the build.
+
+---
+
+## 5. `stock[plr]` can be `nil`, and the type hides it
+
+**Where:** line 40 (type), lines 96-97, 106-107, 139 (reads), lines 142-148
+(setup).
+
+**What's wrong:**
+
+- `PlayerAdded` is connected at the end of the script. Players who are
+  already in the game when it runs (common in Studio playtests, and possible
+  after a slow require) never get a `stock` entry. Every remote they fire then
+  errors when it indexes `nil`.
+- A remote that arrives after `PlayerRemoving` hits the same error.
+- The map is typed `{ [Player]: { [string]: number } }`. Luau treats a lookup
+  in that type as always present, so the checker never flags the missing
+  case.
+
+**What to do instead:** Type it as `{ [Player]: { [BlockType]: number }? }`
+and guard each read (`local playerStock = stock[player]; if not playerStock
+then return end`). After connecting `PlayerAdded`, run the same handler for
+every player in `Players:GetPlayers()`. Once item 2 moves loading to join
+time, this becomes the "player data not loaded yet" guard as well.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Players can never turn receipts off
+
+**Where:** line 99.
+
+**What's wrong:** `plr:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts") or DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS`
+replaces `false` with `true`, because `or` treats `false` like `nil`. Setting
+the attribute to `false` has no effect. `AutoAnchor` (lines 118-121) handles
+this correctly with `== nil`.
+
+**What to do instead:** Use the same pattern as `AutoAnchor`, and check the
+type, since `GetAttribute` returns `any`:
+
+```lua
+local showReceipts = player:GetAttribute("ShowReceipts")
+if typeof(showReceipts) ~= "boolean" then
+	showReceipts = DEFAULT_SHOW_RECEIPTS
+end
+```
+
+Pull this into one small helper, such as
+`getBooleanSetting(player, name, default)`, so both settings read the same
+way.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Unanchored blocks can be moved by clients
+
+**Where:** lines 118-124 and `placeBlock` line 71.
+
+**What's wrong:** When `AutoAnchor` is `false`, the block is unanchored, and
+Roblox gives physics ownership to a nearby client. That client (including an
+exploiter) can then move the block anywhere, push it into other players'
+builds, or fling it. Blocks also drift off the grid, so the overlap check in
+`PlacementRules` stops matching what's in the world.
+
+**What to do instead:** If unanchored blocks are a real feature, call
+`part:SetNetworkOwner(nil)` after parenting so the server simulates them, and
+accept that cost. If they aren't, drop the setting and always anchor.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Clients can flood the server log
+
+**Where:** line 79 (`print` on every placement) and line 114 (`warn` on every
+rejected placement).
+
+**What's wrong:** Both lines run on each remote call, so any client can fill
+the server output by firing the remote over and over. That buries real errors
+and costs time on every placement.
+
+**What to do instead:** Remove the `print`, or put it behind a debug
+constant. Send rejection reasons back to the client (the placing player wants
+to see "OutOfReach"), not to `warn`. Consider a simple per-player rate limit
+on all three remotes.
+
+---
+
+## 9. Function and data shape
+
+**Where:** `placeBlock` (lines 59-82), `PRICES` (lines 14-20),
+`getOwnerName` (line 50), remote creation (lines 24-34).
+
+- **`placeBlock` takes five parameters, two of them switch booleans.** The
+  call `placeBlock(plr, center, bt, autoAnchor, true)` doesn't say what
+  `true` means. Remove `log` (see item 8). Pass `anchored` in an options table
+  (`options: { Anchored: boolean? }?`), or always anchor (see item 7).
+- **`PRICES` repeats the block list from `BlockTypes`.** It's typed
+  `{ [string]: number }`, so adding a block type to `BlockTypes` without a
+  price only shows up later as a runtime error. Type it as
+  `{ [BlockTypes.BlockType]: number }`, so a missing or misspelled entry is a
+  type error. Or move prices into `BlockStyle` / a `BlockPrices` field in
+  `BlockTypes`, so the list lives in one place.
+- **`getOwnerName` has no return type and returns different numbers of values
+  on different paths** (see item 3). If it's kept, give it `: string?` and end
+  with `return nil`.
+- **Remotes are created at runtime.** Client scripts have to `WaitForChild`
+  each name, and nothing shares the names. Define the remotes in
+  `default.project.json` (or create them from one shared module that both
+  sides require), so a typo is caught in one place.
+
+---
+
+## 10. Style (Roblox Lua style guide)
+
+All low impact, and easiest to fix in the same pass as the items above.
+StyLua is configured (`stylua.toml`), so run it after editing.
+
+- **Line 1:** add `--!strict`. Several items above (the untyped
+  `getOwnerName` return, the cast at line 66) are things strict mode would
+  surface. The header comment moves to line 2.
+- **Lines 3-8, 10-22:** bind services, requires, and constants with `const`.
+- **Names:** spell them out. Rename `plr` to `player`, `amt` to `amount`,
+  `bt` to `blockType`, `pos` to `position`, and `p` to `otherPlayer`.
+- **Yielding functions:** after item 2 they may disappear, but any function
+  that calls the DataStore takes an `Async` suffix
+  (`loadCoinsAsync`).
+- **Handlers:** consider named local functions (`onBuyBlocks`,
+  `onPlaceBlock`, `onRemoveBlock`, `onPlayerAdded`, `onPlayerRemoving`). The
+  connections then read as a list at the bottom, and `onPlayerAdded` can be
+  reused for the existing-players loop in item 5.
+- **Booleans:** `showReceipts` and `autoAnchor` read fine as settings. If
+  they become fields, name them as questions (`ShouldShowReceipts`) or keep
+  them matched to the attribute names.
